!! Dynamically adding adapter settings

It feels as if we were close to what we try to achieve. Our focus now is this method in =EDASSettings=:

% ${changes:path=code/001-EDASSettings_class_edaSettingsOn_for_under_.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> edaSettingsOn: aBuilder for: anEDAApplication under: aSymbol

	| parent parentGroup |
	parent := anEDAApplication applicationName.
	parentGroup := aBuilder group: parent.
	parentGroup
		label: parent translated;
		parent: aSymbol;
		description: parent;
		noOrdering.
	self
		environmentSettingsOn: aBuilder
		under: parent
		on: anEDAApplication
		order: 1.
	self
		commandListenerSettingsOn: aBuilder
		under: parent
		on: anEDAApplication
		order: 2.
	self
		portSettingsOn: aBuilder
		under: parent
		on: anEDAApplication
		order: 3
]]]

!!! Making dynamic the simplest case

The first method we want to make dynamic is the following.

% ${changes:path=code/002-EDASSettings_class_environmentSettingsOn_under_on_.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> environmentSettingsOn: aBuilder under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #currentEnvironment
		labelled: 'Environment'
		withDescription: 'Environment'
		usingConfig: app environmentSettings
		under: aParent
		on: app
		order: anInteger.
	app
		notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: nil
		to: app environmentSettings currentEnvironment
]]]

We'd need to add the following information as part of the port metadata:
- the attribute name (=#currentEnvironment=
- the port name (='Environment')
- the port description (='Environment' in this case)
- a block to access the settings instance from the =EDAApplication= itself ('app environmentSettings')
- an optional hook to perform additional logic (=app notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom:to:= in this case)

The new method, as far as we can tell right now, could be as simple as the following.

% ${changes:path=code/003-EDASSettings_class_adapterSettingsOn_for_under_on_order_.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> edaSettingsOn: aBuilder for: anEDAApplication under: aSymbol
EDASSettings class >> adapterSettingsOn: aBuilder for: aPort under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: aPort selector
		labelled: aPort label
		withDescription: aPort description
		usingConfig: app environmentSettings
		under: aParent
		on: app
		order: anInteger.
	aPort settingsBlock value: app
]]]

Focusing on the environment adapter, the method can be rewritten as:

% ${changes:path=code/004-EDASSettings_class_environmentSettingsOn_under_on_.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> edaSettingsOn: aBuilder for: anEDAApplication under: aSymbol

	| parent parentGroup |
	parent := anEDAApplication applicationName.
	parentGroup := aBuilder group: parent.
	parentGroup
		label: parent translated;
		parent: aSymbol;
		description: parent;
		noOrdering.
	self
		adapterSettingsOn: aBuilder
		for: EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort
		under: parent
		on: anEDAApplication
		order: 1.
	self
		portSettingsOn: aBuilder
		under: parent
		on: anEDAApplication
		order: 2
]]

Now it's just a matter of implementing the new methods in =EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort=.

% ${changes:path=code/005-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_class_selector.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort class >> selector

	^ #currentEnvironment
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/006-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_class_label.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort class >> label

	^ 'Environment'
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/007-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_class_description.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort class >> description

	^ 'Environment'
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/007-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_class_description.ombu}$
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort class >> settingsBlock

	^ [ :app |
	  app
		  notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: nil
		  to: app environmentSettings currentEnvironment ]
]]]

If we open a new SettingsBrowser, it should display the environment adapter as before.

!!! Making dynamic a non-trivial case

For the command line adapter, the method to export its attributes as settings is more involved.

[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> commandListenerSettingsOn: aBuilder under: parent on: app order: anInteger

	| key |
	key := parent , 'commandListener'.
	(aBuilder group: key)
		label: 'Command Listener' translated;
		description: 'Command listener';
		parent: parent;
		order: anInteger.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #host
		labelled: 'Host'
		withDescription: 'Stomp host address / IP'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 1.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #port
		labelled: 'Port'
		withDescription: 'Stomp host port'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 2.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #user
		labelled: 'User'
		withDescription: 'Stomp user'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 3.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #password
		labelled: 'Password'
		withDescription: 'Stomp password'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 4.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #queue
		labelled: 'Queue'
		withDescription: 'Stomp queue'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 5.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #timeout
		labelled: 'Timeout'
		withDescription: 'Stomp timeout'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 5.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #prefetchCount
		labelled: 'Prefetch count'
		withDescription: 'The number of messages to prefetch'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 6.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #durable
		labelled: 'Durable'
		withDescription: 'Whether the topic is durable'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 7.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #debug
		labelled: 'Debug'
		withDescription: 'Whether to enable debug mode'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 8.
	self
		commandListenerErrorStrategySettingsOn: aBuilder
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 9.
	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #restartOnErrors
		labelled: 'Restart on errors'
		withDescription: 'Whether to restart the listener in case of errors'
		usingConfig: app commandListenerSettings
		under: key
		on: app
		order: 10
]]]

Basically, we need a way to iterate over the adapter's attributes. We could annotate their accessor methods, but first we need to be able to distinguish between the simple and not-so-simple cases.
